{
  "name": "Planning a Bathroom Addition: Steps from Rough-In to Fixtures",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1: Establish drain slopes and sewer line connection points",
      "text": "Identify your home's main soil stack or existing sewer line to confirm that new waste pipes can achieve the required slope of one-quarter inch per foot. If you are adding a bathroom on a slab foundation or far from existing drain runs, trenching or alternative routing will be necessary. Establishing waste line elevation first ensures you do not design fixtures you cannot legally drain.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 2: Plan fixture clearances and functional floor layouts",
      "text": "Map out exact clearances for toilets, showers, vanities, and entry doors according to local building codes. Ensure you have the required open floor space in front of each fixture and adequate distance from the center of the toilet to adjacent walls. Allow room for swing radiuses on shower doors, vanity drawers, and entryways.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 2
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 3: Secure trade permits and prepare mechanical pathways",
      "text": "Submit layout drawings and plumbing plans to your local building department for review and permit approval. Plan the exact path for your exhaust ductwork, which must vent directly outdoors rather than discharging into an attic, soffit, or crawlspace. Secure all necessary electrical, mechanical, and plumbing permits before starting structural demolition or framing.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 3
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 4: Execute rough framing, plumbing, and electrical rough-ins",
      "text": "Frame partition walls, backer blocking for grab bars or floating vanities, and recessed niches. Plumbers install supply piping, drain pipes, cleanout access points, and shower valves, while electricians run dedicated 20-amp GFCI circuits. Schedule your official rough-in inspection so code officials can verify pipe slope, venting, and wiring before walls are covered.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 4
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 5: Apply professional waterproofing systems",
      "text": "Install cement backer board or approved waterproof foam panels across all shower and tub wet zones. Apply continuous liquid or sheet waterproofing membranes around shower pans, curbs, seams, and valve cutouts to prevent structural rot. Complete a water test on the shower pan if required by your jurisdiction to prove a watertight seal.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 5
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 6: Install tile, set finish fixtures, and test operations",
      "text": "Lay your chosen floor and wall tile, grout thoroughly, and apply appropriate sealers. Mount the vanity, sink, toilet, shower trim, glass enclosure, mirror, and light fixtures. Test all water supplies, drainage speeds, and exhaust fan airflow rates before scheduling the final building inspection. Common Mistakes to Avoid Never vent high-moisture exhaust fans into an attic space, which invites mold and dry-rot in roof framing. Avoid placing plumbing valves behind permanently sealed walls without providing accessible service points. For dependable bathroom additions backed by skilled craftsmanship, reach out to the design-build team at Hometelligent Inc. at 925-383-8163.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 6
    }
  ],
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "speakable": {
    "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
    "cssSelector": [
      "h1",
      ".howto-step",
      "[itemprop=\"step\"]"
    ]
  },
  "totalTime": "PT30M",
